Subject: [PATCH] kernel/fs: unlock a new I/O context before freeing it on
 failure

Since c02707824b ("kernel/fs: Add some more lock assertions"),
vfs_new_io_context() takes the new context's lock with a WriteLocker for
the whole function. When vfs_resize_fd_table() fails, the context is
freed and NULL returned, and only then does the WriteLocker's destructor
run: it unlocks an rw_lock inside freed memory.

That path is taken when the FD table cannot be allocated, which happens
under memory pressure -- fork() of a team with a large FD table limit.
On a RenkuOS arm64 VM running several Chromium processes, with the
syslog at "low resource memory: critical":

  PANIC: acquire_spinlock(): Failed to acquire spinlock ... (value: deadbeef)
  acquire_spinlock
  _rw_lock_write_unlock
  vfs_new_io_context
  fork_team
  syscall_dispatcher

Release the lock, destroy it, and then free the context.

Signed-off-by: rainygirl <rainygirl@gmail.com>

Upstream: https://github.com/RenkuOS/Source (branch fork-io-context-oom, PR pending)
---
 src/system/kernel/fs/vfs.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/fs/vfs.cpp b/src/system/kernel/fs/vfs.cpp
index 2fad5000e9..9688511b94 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/fs/vfs.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/fs/vfs.cpp
@@ -5047,6 +5047,10 @@ vfs_new_io_context(const io_context* parentContext, bool purgeCloseOnExec)
 		tableSize = DEFAULT_FD_TABLE_SIZE;
 
 	if (vfs_resize_fd_table(context, tableSize) != B_OK) {
+		// contextLocker holds the lock inside the context; release it before
+		// the context goes, or its destructor unlocks freed memory.
+		contextLocker.Unlock();
+		rw_lock_destroy(&context->lock);
 		free(context);
 		return NULL;
 	}
-- 
